menu "Kiki ESP32"

config KIKI_WIFI_SSID
    string "2.4 GHz Wi-Fi SSID"
    default ""

config KIKI_WIFI_PASSWORD
    string "Wi-Fi password"
    default ""

config KIKI_WIFI_FORCE_BUILTIN
    bool "Use only the built-in network, ignoring anything saved on the panel"
    default n
    help
        Normally the board joins the network saved on its panel and only falls
        back to the built-in one above when that fails -- a deliberate safety
        net, because a saved network can be wrong rather than merely absent and
        a board that can never reach a gateway is a board nobody can fix
        remotely.

        Turn this on to pin the board to the built-in network and nothing else.
        It exists for testing on a deliberately bad link: the fallback is what
        rescues the board onto the house Wi-Fi the moment the weak AP drops,
        which silently turns a bad-link test into a good-link one.

        With this set there is no rescue. If the built-in network is out of
        range the board goes to the provisioning screen and stays there.

config KIKI_GATEWAY_URI
    string "Laptop gateway WebSocket URI"
    default "ws://192.168.1.10:8765"

config KIKI_GATEWAY_URI_FALLBACK
    string "Public gateway URI, tried when the LAN one fails"
    default ""
    help
        A publicly reachable wss:// URL for the same gateway -- a Tailscale
        Funnel or Cloudflare Tunnel hostname -- so Kiki still works away from
        home. Leave empty to only ever use the LAN address.

        The LAN URI is always tried first and is always preferred, because
        this one leaves the building: audio streams continuously at 100
        frames a second, and routing that through a public ingress adds
        latency to the whole voice loop for no benefit when the gateway is
        one hop away.

        Anything reachable from the public internet MUST have
        KIKI_GATEWAY_TOKEN set. The gateway accepts any client when no token
        is configured, and this endpoint carries audio, inference and tool
        access.

config KIKI_GATEWAY_TOKEN
    string "Gateway device token"
    default ""

config KIKI_AUDIO_VOLUME
    int "Speaker volume percent"
    range 0 100
    default 100

config KIKI_AEC_ENABLED
    bool "Full-duplex acoustic echo cancellation and voice barge-in"
    default y
    help
        Continuously processes both microphones and the board's dedicated
        analog speaker-reference channel through ESP-SR's high-performance
        full-duplex AEC. Clean 16 kHz audio continues to the gateway while the
        speaker is active, where a second conservative VAD confirms a real
        nearby voice before interrupting playback.

        If AEC cannot initialize, firmware fails closed: ordinary listening
        remains available, but raw microphone audio is not sent during
        playback and therefore cannot self-trigger on Kiki's voice.

config KIKI_CHARGE_CURRENT_MA
    int "Battery charge current (mA)"
    range 0 1000
    default 500
    help
        The constant-current phase limit written to the AXP2101 (register
        0x62). This MUST track the cell: the PMIC cannot detect capacity, so
        nothing else will catch a mismatch.

        Set it to about 0.5C for the cell fitted -- 500 mA for a 1050 mAh
        cell, 400 mA for 800 mAh, 150 mA for 300 mAh. 1C is the usual maximum
        rating; above 0.5C the cell runs warmer and loses cycle life, and
        above 1C you are outside what most cells are rated for.

        The chip has discrete steps (25 mA to 200 mA, then 100 mA), and the
        firmware rounds DOWN to the nearest one so it never charges harder
        than asked. The board's default was 200 mA.

        Note the VBUS input limit (1000 mA) covers the system load as well as
        charging, so beyond ~500 mA the input limit throttles charging while
        the board is running -- and a laptop USB port may supply far less
        than that.

config KIKI_CHARGE_LIMIT_PERCENT
    int "Stop battery charging at this percentage"
    range 50 100
    default 95
    help
        Stops the AXP2101 cell charger when its fuel gauge reaches this level.
        Charging is enabled again five percentage points below the limit so a
        noisy gauge cannot rapidly switch the charger on and off near the
        boundary. The default therefore stops at 95% and resumes at 90%.

    config KIKI_LOW_POWER
        bool "Dim the screen when running from a battery"
        default n
        help
            Dims the AMOLED, and nothing else. It used to enable Wi-Fi modem
            sleep as well; that is gone, on battery too. Dozing between DTIM
            beacons makes every inbound packet wait for the next wake, and a
            conversational device that answers late has failed at the only
            thing anyone judges it on. The radio now stays awake on any power
            source -- see wifi_station_start().

            So this no longer trades latency for runtime. It trades brightness
            for runtime, which costs nothing you can hear.

            This does NOT reduce the boot surge. Display init and Wi-Fi init
            both draw hard within the first second, and a cell that cannot
            source roughly an amp will brown out regardless of any setting
            here. That is a property of the cell's internal resistance, not of
            the firmware.

    config KIKI_LOW_POWER_BRIGHTNESS
        int "Screen brightness when dimmed (%)"
        depends on KIKI_LOW_POWER
        range 5 100
        default 30

endmenu
